    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (the `AcceptanceTest` is the only test present, and it passes).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `namesakeCount` field on owner creation, following the existing pattern used for `customerCode` and `householdId`.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `namesakeCount` integer property to the `Owner` schema, so the OpenAPI-generated `OwnerDto` exposes it.\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added a `namesakeCount` field (`@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter, so JPA persists it and MapStruct maps it to the DTO automatically.\n\n3. **`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 In `addOwner`, compute the count *before* saving via a new `countNamesakes(firstName, lastName)` helper that counts existing owners matching both names case-insensitively (using `equalsIgnoreCase`). The value is fixed at creation time, persisted, and returned on later `GET`s.\n\n4. **`db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`** \u2014 Added the `namesake_count` column to the `owners` table (the tests run on the `hsqldb` profile with `ddl-auto=none`, so the schema column is required).\n\nFor the test scenario: creating owner *b* (same first/last name as *a*, different postcode \u2192 different household) records `namesakeCount = 1`, which the `GET` then returns.",
